{

suppose (^E G) is an E-group for G ([ABV], Def 4.6) (y,Lambda) is an
ABV Langlands parameter. We can find a Cartan (^vee H) in (^vee G)
\subset (^E G), a Borel (^vee B) containing (^vee H), and lambda \in
(^vee h), so that

1) Lambda is the canonical flat of lambda
2) y \in N((^vee H) and in nonidentity component of (^vee E), acting on (^vee H)
   by an automorphism (^vee theta)
3) y^2 = exp(2\pi i \lambda) = e(Lambda)
4) lambda is weakly dominant for (^vee B)
5) if av is a positive root vanishing on lambda, then (^vee theta)(av) is
   nonnegative on lambda
6) {some condition about imaginary/real roots??}

This makes y = t sigma(w) delta, with delta the distinguished involution, sigma(w)
the Tits representative of a Weyl group element w, and t in H. Furthermore

t = e((lambda-(^vee theta)*lambda)/2 + dtf. We want to make y a KGBElt for the
group vE, as an element of the extended group (^E (^vee E)). So we need to fix
a class of distinguished involutions in (^E (^vee E)). Factor

(^vee theta) = vw_E * delta_E,

with vw_E in W(^vee E) and delta_E an involution of the based root datum of (^vee E).
We need to choose an element delta_E of (^L G) that does this. Start by factoring

delta_E = vx * delta,

with vx in W(G) (normalizing E). Then try

delta_{E,0} = (Tits extension of vx)*delta.

I think this element must have square equal to e((1-delta_{E,0})rho_check) =
e(sum of coroots) (and so has order 2 or 4), and it acts on (^vee H) as required,
but it need NOT (claude points out) act by +1 on the delta_E-fixed simple roots.
I don't see how to explicitly to correct to delta_E, and so I don't know how to compute
(delta_E)^2, which is needed to replace rho_E in the endo_claude.

Once this correction is made, (delta_E)^2 is something e(r_E), with r_E integral for
(^vee E) but NOT NECESSARILY EQUAL rho_check_E. The definition of endo_claude(p) needs
not rho_shift but rho - r_E, which changes the integrality requirement on lambda_minus.
With these adjustments, the formula I suggested for claude_shift ought to work.
}

{
Here is an idea. Suppose G is quasisplit. For each twisted involution theta, there is a
unique KGBElt x(theta) with this involution and torus factor zero. (check this!) Among these,
there is a unique x(delta) of length zero, attached to the distinguished involution. You can
move among these special x(theta) by complex cross actions and downward Cayley through simple
imaginary roots (which are always defined, since for these x the simple imaginary are all
noncompact). For upward Cayley, when it is double-valued, just one has torus factor zero.}



{
Given p = parameter(x,lambda,nu), replace x by x1 = KGB_elt(p.inner_class, p.x.involution,
 0*p.lambda). This is a "base" KGBElt in which all simple imaginary roots are noncompact. This gives
 p1 = parameter(x,lambda,nu), for the quasisplit inner form of G, but with the same infinitesimal character
 and the same integrality datum.
 }